ike 18  years ago and you know about what can I  say now everything is ok that's that's  the main thing yeah so and I really  think that it's going to be ok for the  next I know forever they have quite  different concerns such as whether  they'll be able to earn a living from  their music when born er Luka and the  others think about their future after  graduation it doesn't look too good  more than 40 percent of young Croatians  are unemployed something they have in  common with many other young Europeans  Boehner says without work experience you  can't even get an entry-level job when  we finish college no one wants us a lot  of companies employ people who've been  with them for twenty thirty years  already they retire but keep on working  for less money the companies exploit  them and we are completely forgotten  about  at least they've got their music but  even during the soundcheck there's  trouble a neighbor comes to complain  about the noise  it seems to me that conflict between the  War generation and the post-war  generation are routine in Croatia to see  how many traces of the war in the 1990s  are left I take a drive along the former  front line between Croatia and Serbia  abandoned villages desolate landscapes  in eastern Croatian Slavonia before the  wall it was mostly Serbs who lived here  after the Croatian army liberated the  occupied region thousands of Serbs fled  there are a lot of houses for sale  economically this region is yet to  recover from the war there is very  little work available the jobs of Marco  pizu llyich and his colleagues are safe  but also extremely dangerous there  minesweepers we drive to a small town  called Ratko vats serbian troops were  stationed nearby during the war and they  mined the nearby woods Marko and his  team are here to clear the mines a  laborious task you can see here what  we've done already the square meters we  did yesterday says Marko  today we'll move on to this area  everyone has to sign for the area  t